@doc <PREFIX>-OVERVIEW [ref, active, agents+employees, machine-drafted] v1
## <One dense line: what this repo is, its runtime, and the one thing that surprises people. This is often the only string an agent reads before deciding what to fetch.>
@links <PREFIX>-ARCH,<PREFIX>-DEPS,<PREFIX>-STANDARDS
@anchor [repo:<repo-name>, vcs:git]
  + <entrypoint module glob>
  + infrastructure/**
  + config/**
  + README.md
@cycle 90d
@reviewed <YYYY-MM-DD from: git log -1 --format=%ad --date=short -- <anchored paths>>

@def <slug>
  Continuous prose, one primary definition. What this service or library is, what it receives,
  what it produces, and what it delegates. Say what it is in the system, not what its files are.
  Three to six lines. Not bullets.

@props
  + runtime        | <language + version>
  + <module>       | <its job in one line>
  + <module>       | <its job in one line>
  + entrypoints    | <composition root; config catalogs; IaC root>
  + <notable>      | <anything an agent must know before writing code here>

@boundary
  + owns     | <responsibility this repo genuinely owns>
  + owns     | <another>
  ! not-owns | <responsibility people assume it owns> -> <where it actually lives>
  ! not-owns | <another> -> <owner>

@status
  <key>: <what is actually true right now, including the embarrassing parts>
  known-gap: <in-memory store, unbuilt ADR, disabled fallback — an agent that does not know this writes wrong code>
